78 1078 #edb/ref 17592186441557 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441578 1079 #uuid "e210b2c8-4214-56d5-9adf-4f596e3021f5" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441579 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441545 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441579 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441554 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441579 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441555 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441579 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441561 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441579 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441569 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441579 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441579 1077 "selection" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441579 1078 #edb/ref 17592186441569 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441579 1079 #uuid "eb9967eb-bff2-51e3-a27a-d037cf218913" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441572 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441580 1069 "q-316438" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441580 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441580 1071 "Suppose that $|\\,x\\,|=9$, $|\\,y\\,|=6$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta =\\pi .$ Find the vector projection of $x$ onto $y$ in terms of the vector $y.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{y}\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$y$" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441580 1072 #uuid "24948042-4caf-54ad-85ae-20d1f75949ff" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441580 1074 "First, we find the scalar projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{y}\\,x & =|\\,x\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $y:$\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{|\\,y\\,|}=\\frac{y}{6}=\\frac{1}{6}y\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the vector projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{y}\\,x & ={\\text{comp}}_{y}\\,x\\cdot u \\\\  & =-9\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}y \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441573 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441581 1069 "q-212463" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441581 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441581 1071 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ x \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Given that the projection of $a$ onto $b$ is the zero vector, find the value of $x.$" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441581 1072 #uuid "25a56934-09b0-592a-b002-4f360029a2ea" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441581 1074 "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals the zero vector only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 11+4\\cdot x+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4x+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ 4x+6 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =-6 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441579 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441582 1069 "q-101538" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441582 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441582 1071 "Suppose that $|\\,c\\,|=\\sqrt{2}$, $|\\,d\\,|=6$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta ={45}^{∘}.$ Find the vector projection of $d$ onto $c$ in terms of the vector $c.$" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441582 1072 #uuid "30aa8aaa-a9f8-52a5-8c3a-51fa1949b560" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441582 1074 "First, we find the scalar projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{c}\\,d & =|\\,d\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $c:$\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{c}{|\\,c\\,|}=\\frac{c}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}c\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the vector projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{c}\\,d & ={\\text{comp}}_{c}\\,d\\cdot u \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}c \\\\  & =3c\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441574 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441583 1069 "q-101545" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441583 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441583 1071 "Let $c=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $d=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find the vector projection of $d$ onto $c.$" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441583 1072 #uuid "35d9ba0b-2c2a-5791-9dcc-879b93c51511" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441583 1074 "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{c}\\,d & =\\frac{c\\cdot d}{c\\cdot c}c \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{{c}_{x}^{2}+{c}_{y}^{2}+{c}_{z}^{2}}c \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 3+5\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 4}{{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}c \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{26}⟨1,5,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{7}{26},-\\frac{35}{26},0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441584 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441578 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441584 1069 "q-19212" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441584 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441584 1071 "Let $p=⟨2,1,6⟩$ and $q=⟨4,2,12⟩.$ Find the vector projection of $p$ onto $q.$" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441584 1072 #uuid "66d2028c-7755-5929-9531-f282efb6ee05" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441584 1074 "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q}\\,p & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{q\\cdot q}q \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}q \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 12}{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{12}^{2}}q \\\\  & =\\frac{82}{164}⟨4,2,12⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨4,2,12⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441577 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441585 1069 "q-275227" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441585 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441585 1071 "Suppose that $|\\,a\\,|=16$, $|\\,b\\,|=2$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Find the vector projection of $a$ onto $b$ in terms of the vector $b.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a=$\n{{field-1}}\n$b$" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441585 1072 #uuid "8e5a64ce-22c5-5e8c-997a-c2e3826ce849" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441585 1074 "First, we find the scalar projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $b:$\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{b}{|\\,b\\,|}=\\frac{b}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}b\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the vector projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & ={\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a\\cdot u \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}b \\\\  & =-4\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441571 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441586 1069 "q-126506" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441586 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441586 1071 "Suppose that $|\\,c\\,|=16$, $|\\,d\\,|=4$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta ={60}^{∘}.$ Find the vector projection of $c$ onto $d$ in terms of the vector $d.$" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441586 1072 #uuid "a12537f5-cc57-59f7-b432-1391f413c86b" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441586 1074 "First, we find the scalar projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c & =|\\,c\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{cos}⁡({60}^{∘}) \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $d:$\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{d}{|\\,d\\,|}=\\frac{d}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}d\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the vector projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{d}\\,c & ={\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c\\cdot u \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}d \\\\  & =2d\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441576 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441587 1069 "q-19213" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441587 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441587 1071 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the vector projection of $b$ onto $a.$" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441587 1072 #uuid "c0976e49-e373-59e5-b244-6a3f86c33d78" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441587 1074 "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{a\\cdot a}a \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 14+2\\cdot (-1)}{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}⟨3,0,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{13},0,\\frac{2}{13}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441575 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441588 1069 "q-212469" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441588 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441588 1071 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ Given that the projection of $a$ onto $b$ is the zero vector, find the value of $x.$" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441588 1072 #uuid "fc72b4b3-1726-5899-88e5-9bd9e302a6a9" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true] [17592186441588 1074 "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals the zero vector only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot x+(-4)\\cdot 5+1\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =4x-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ 4x-24 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =24 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534863 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-086de454-2713-5601-ab51-799550e139d7" 17592186441534 "answer-0ae82f23-3f33-55f8-b143-c4e2aa44358a" 17592186441535 "answer-0b5a1709-5c52-5848-bfee-e95ed0c3f88a" 17592186441536 "answer-192f30d7-4e5b-597c-b16e-383b0b641a0d" 17592186441537 "answer-23855489-4a31-5f1e-b065-d3158a58159b" 17592186441538 "answer-27703fdc-dcae-5810-af67-bcf1ab7b205c" 17592186441539 "answer-2ff64492-2e92-58ae-94ff-14ee9bcf8e67" 17592186441540 "answer-332eee39-a81f-58e2-9df9-1c8788ae1cd9" 17592186441541 "answer-3a7d4837-c858-547a-810b-4a932bb463f5" 17592186441542 "answer-47726351-7b47-5626-bb7b-c86df88ec37f" 17592186441543 "answer-4898f0e7-39a5-5f06-8e56-8f5aafed918a" 17592186441544 "answer-4f759721-d199-5792-92d1-a10cf5a4cae6" 17592186441545 "answer-5f7f0f17-57fa-52b7-993d-3b6b9a55e712" 17592